Subject: [PATCH 7/8] RR: route the HYBRID keyswitch through RRChain (rotations
 reach it); evk-shape guard
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Last milestone (b) deferred item, partially landed.

ROUTED. KeySwitchHYBRID::KeySwitchCore now dispatches to RRChain::KeySwitchCore
when IsRRChain(). Hooking here rather than overriding EvalAutomorphism covers
EVERY keyswitch — rotations, automorphisms, relin — with one guard, and it is
exactly where the rotation path was landing. RRChain gains RRLevelOfElement(e),
which recovers the level from an element's MODULI: the keyswitch entry point is
handed a bare DCRTPoly with no ciphertext to ask, and limb count alone does not
identify an RR level.

CONFIRMED WORKING: the backtrace now shows EvalRotate -> EvalAtIndex ->
EvalAutomorphism -> KeySwitchHYBRID::KeySwitchCore -> RRChain::KeySwitchCore, so
the generic prefix-chain path is out of the rotation route.

NOT FINISHED, and the remaining question is now exact rather than vague. Inside
RRChain::KeySwitchCore a rotation key fails an operand check that a relin key
passes (test_rr_keyswitch is bit-exact over 9 windows). Added a guard that states
it with numbers instead of letting PolyImpl report "Modulus missmatch":

  level r=26 window [0,47], digit 0, part 0/4
  wants global row 0 of 61      (= 48 Q + 13 P, so the key IS full-chain at P*Qmax)
  tower modulus 36175873  vs  evk row modulus 41680897

Those are chain[4] and chain[6]. NEITHER is chain[0], which is what a window
starting at lo=0 should yield on both sides. So the key's SHAPE is right and this
is an ORDERING/indexing question, not a missing or mis-generated key:
- the positional read `row = (i < sizeW) ? lvl.lo + i : sizeQglobal + (i - sizeW)`
  assumes the evk's Q rows are in RR CHAIN LAYOUT order, which is what the
  RRChain constructor asserts of GetElementParams();
- the digit tower's modulus comes from dg.paramsD, so the same question applies
  to whether cPart's towers are being read in layout order.
Resolving which of the two is off-order is the next step; both are one lookup.

Rotation keys are also generated by the STANDARD keygen path, which milestone (b)
never made RR-aware — so it is worth checking that EvalAtIndexKeyGen produces the
same single-key-at-P*Qmax convention the RR evk uses before assuming the reader
is at fault.

Still NOT installed into deps_n32.
